            <div xml:id="d498">

                <pb facs="0501" n="491" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg491"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.2231/121: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Ecuador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sparks</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-02-18T18:00:00-05:00">February 18, 1937—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[February 19—7:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>10. Referring to Department’s telegram No. 6, February 17, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="37"
                        xml:id="d498-f1"/> naranjilla is the fruit of the solananum Quito enselam
                    apparently native of Ecuador. It is a solanaceous rather than a citrus product.
                    It is planned to export the juice in concentrated form for use as a
                    non-alcoholic beverage.</p>
                <p>Further information may be obtainable from the special report by Crilley to the
                    Department of Commerce in 1935.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sparks</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
